@@ -140,6 +140,26 @@ boxes marked for destruction are skipped, as the snapshot would have done.
 the oracle. `make verify-gameplay-collision-owner` checks the prefix against
 newborn, destroyed and replacement boxes, and armed boxes beside idle ones.
 
+Native enemy alarms borrow `GameplayWorld`'s cached Step ordering, captured
+at the alarm boundary. This removes the second enemy handle capture; it does
+not borrow the compact enemy record array, which can move on retirement.
+Each entry still holds its original identity slot and generation. The merge
+visits enemy entries at their captured resource-ID position among the other
+families, with boxes at object 92, for each alarm index. It reads live flags
+and event-presence metadata before the existing typed countdown adapter.
+That adapter resolves the live lifetime and handles inactive/destruction
+marks and negative counters. Its indices above 1 are unchanged no-ops (this
+does not claim the original game lacks those events).
+
+The borrow lasts only through the bound combat alarm callback. None of its
+implemented enemy, box, debris, time-change, drone or entry-dither handlers
+starts another Step or captures enemy Step order. Birth/retirement invalidates
+the revision without overwriting this snapshot; the later Step capture can
+then refresh it. Arbitrary caller-owned `dispatchAlarmPhase` callbacks retain
+the generic snapshot. `REFERENCE_ENEMY_ALARMS=1` retains the room capture on
+both targets. The alarm fixtures compare all three ownership modes, counter
+rearming, frog chaining, slot reuse/compaction and every RNG state word.
+
